/* ML Academy · THE EXPOSITION (English)
   Same keys as anlatim.js: 'lessonId:stepNo'. Read before touching the model. */
var ANLATIM_EN = {
  'veri:1': '<p>Machine learning starts with a table. That sounds ordinary, but the whole field rests on it: <b>we show the world to a model as a table</b>. However complex a problem is, if it cannot be put into that shape the model cannot see it.</p><p>A table has two directions and they say different things. The <b>rows</b> tell you who was measured and the <b>columns</b> what was measured. In a table of students every row is a student and every column a piece of information about that student.</p><p>That distinction looks trivial but it directly determines what a model can learn. A model looks for a <b>pattern</b> across the rows: "when this column is large, is that one large too?" For the question to be askable at all, the table has to be built properly first.</p>',
  'veri:2': '<p>The columns of a table are not equal. Some are information we <b>give</b> the model and one is the answer we <b>ask</b> of it.</p><p>What we give is called a <b>feature</b> and is usually written x. What we ask for is called the <b>label</b>, written y. If you are predicting the price of a house, the floor area, the number of rooms and the district are features; the price is the label.</p><p>You make that split, not the data. Ask a different question of the same table and a different column becomes the label. So <b>the label is a property of the question, not of the data</b>.</p><p>And the critical point: the label is the thing you do <b>not</b> have at prediction time. If you have it there is nothing to predict. That sentence looks simple, but in the data leakage lesson it turns out to be the source of a whole class of errors.</p>',
  'veri:3': '<p>The columns told you what was measured. The <b>rows tell you who</b>. Every row is a single observation: one student, one transaction, one patient, one photograph.</p><p>A model cannot learn from a single row. One row only says "this student worked 6 hours and scored 66"; that is a fact, not a rule. A rule only appears when you look at <b>many rows at once</b>: "as study hours grow the score grows."</p><p>This is why the size of a dataset is not an incidental detail. Every new row wipes a little more noise off the rule you are looking for. A tendency found in ten rows may be luck; one found in a thousand is probably real.</p>',
  'veri:4': '<p>A table is a correct representation but a bad one for the human eye. Look at ten rows and you will not see the relationship; put the same ten rows on a plot and it appears at a glance.</p><p>The transformation is simple: <b>every row becomes a point</b>. You put a feature on the horizontal axis and the label on the vertical one. Now "as study hours grow the score grows" is no longer a sentence but a <b>rise</b> on your screen.</p><p>Almost every plot you see in this course rests on that transformation. And a model does the same thing: it looks at the points and searches for a rule that runs through them.</p>',
  'veri:5': '<p>The word "model" is vague in everyday speech. In machine learning its definition is very narrow: <b>a model is a function that turns an input into an output.</b> It contains a few adjustable numbers, and learning means adjusting them.</p><p>In its simplest form it is a line: <code>y = w·x + b</code>. Here x is the input, y the prediction and <b>w and b are the model\'s knowledge</b>. Saying the model "learned" means those two numbers changed to fit the data. There is no other magic.</p><p>A huge language model has the same structure; it just has hundreds of billions of numbers instead of two. This is why the answer to "how does AI learn" is the same at every scale, and the next lesson shows exactly that.</p>',

  'ezber:1': '<p>How do you tell whether a model works? The first answer that comes to mind is wrong: "does it give the right answer on the data I gave it?"</p><p>That criterion is wrong because it cannot tell <b>memorising</b> the data from <b>understanding</b> it. A program that writes your table into memory verbatim and a program that finds the underlying rule both look perfect on that data.</p><p>The two approaches sit side by side here. One remembers every student individually, the other extracts the rule "as study hours grow the score grows". Both look at the same data and both appear to work.</p><p>The only place you can see the difference is a student <b>neither of them has seen</b>. The whole lesson rests on that.</p>',
  'ezber:2': '<p>Testing a model on the data it learned from is like giving a student an exam made of exactly the questions they revised. They get a high mark, and the mark proves nothing.</p><p>A memorising model always gets full marks on that exam, because it has seen every question before and stored the answer. A rule learning model also scores highly, but it does not have to be perfect; a rule need not pass exactly through every point.</p><p>The inversion here matters: <b>the model that looks better on the training data can be the worse model.</b> Because a perfect fit is usually a sign of memorisation rather than understanding.</p><p>This is why machine learning has one rule with no exceptions: <b>test a model on data it did not see while learning.</b></p>',
  'ezber:3': '<p>Now the real question: what will the model say about a student it has never seen?</p><p>The memorising model does not have that student. All it can do is find the closest record it has and return that answer. Sometimes it works, mostly it does not, and nobody can explain why it did or did not.</p><p>The rule learning model applies its formula. Never having seen the new student is not a problem, because it learned a <b>rule</b>, not a <b>list</b>.</p><p>That difference has a name: <b>generalisation</b>, the ability to behave correctly on unseen data. It is the only thing worth measuring in machine learning. Success on the training data means nothing next to it.</p>',
  'ezber:4': '<p>Both failures have standard names and both are dangerous.</p><p><b>Overfitting:</b> the model took the data too seriously and mistook the noise for the rule. Perfect in training, bad on new data. The symptom is clear: a low training error and a high test error.</p><p><b>Underfitting:</b> the model did not take the data seriously at all; it is not flexible enough to catch the rule. This time it is bad in both training and testing.</p><p>These are the two ends of one dial, and <b>there is a sweet spot in the middle</b>. Much of this course is about ways of finding it: penalty terms, cross validation, early stopping. They all answer the same question: how flexible should the model be?</p>',
  'nasil-ogrenir:1': '<p>The previous lesson gave you the definition of a model: a function that turns an input into an output, with a few adjustable numbers inside. Now we come to adjusting those numbers.</p><p>The model here is a line: <code>y = w·x + b</code>. It has two numbers. <b>w is the slope</b>: how much y changes when x grows by one unit. <b>b is the intercept</b>: where the line crosses the vertical axis. Change these two and the line rotates and shifts; it can do nothing else.</p><p>"Learning" means bringing those two numbers to the values that best fit the data. For now <b>you</b> will do it, by hand. The point is to feel how hard the job is: with two numbers trial and error is possible. A language model has billions of numbers and there is no such thing as adjusting them by hand. The rest of this lesson builds the method that automates what you are about to do manually.</p>',
  'nasil-ogrenir:2': '<p>To improve something automatically you first have to be able to <b>measure</b> it. "This line fits the data well" is an impression; you cannot give a computer an impression, you have to give it a number.</p><p>The measurement is built like this. For every point, the difference between the model\'s prediction and the true value is called the <b>residual</b>. Some residuals are positive and some negative; add them directly and they cancel out, making a bad model look good. So each one is <b>squared</b> first: that removes the sign problem and punishes large errors disproportionately.</p><p>The mean of those squares is the <b>mean squared error</b> (MSE). It is a single number and the smaller it is the better the model. Measures like this are called <b>loss functions</b>, and without one the word "learning" has no definition: a system that does not know what to improve can learn nothing.</p>',
  'nasil-ogrenir:3': '<p>You can now compute a single number for every pair (w, b). That means: <b>there is a map of all possible models.</b></p><p>Picture it: w on the horizontal axis, b on the vertical one, and the height of every point is that model\'s loss. A landscape appears. The high ground is bad models and the hollows are good ones. What you are looking for is <b>the lowest point</b>.</p><p>This view redefines the whole field: <b>learning is searching a landscape for its lowest point.</b> Training a model is not a discovery but a search problem.</p><p>There is one catch. The only reason I can draw this map is that there are two parameters. With three you cannot draw it and with a million you cannot even imagine it. In real training <b>the map is invisible.</b> The next step is entirely about that constraint.</p>',
  'nasil-ogrenir:4': '<p>You cannot see the map. So what can you see? <b>The slope at the point you are standing on.</b></p><p>Imagine being on a mountain in fog. You do not know where the valley is, but you can feel which way the ground under your feet tilts. If you want to go down, what to do is clear: take a step in the steepest downhill direction, then look again.</p><p>In mathematics that slope is called the <b>derivative</b>. Because there are two parameters there are two slopes: how much the loss changes in the w direction and in the b direction. The vector formed by those two is called the <b>gradient</b>, and the gradient points in the direction of steepest <b>ASCENT</b>.</p><p>This is why you go in the <b>opposite</b> direction to go down. That is where the method gets its name: gradient descent.</p>',
  'nasil-ogrenir:5': '<p>Now let us take a single step apart. Every step consists of the same three jobs:</p><p><b>1 · Measure.</b> Predict with the current w and b and compute the error. <b>2 · Find the direction.</b> Compute the slope of the loss in the w and b directions. <b>3 · Take a step.</b> Move each parameter a small amount against its own slope.</p><p>The "small amount" in the third step is a multiplier called the <b>learning rate</b> (η). The slope only tells you the <b>direction</b>, not how far to go. That decision is yours.</p><p>The whole thing fits on one line: <code>θ ← θ − η·∇L</code>. θ is the parameters, ∇L the gradient and η the learning rate. That line will come back again and again in this course, because almost every model trained today is trained with it.</p>',
  'nasil-ogrenir:6': '<p>A single step barely changes the model. Its power is in repetition.</p><p>Do the same three jobs over and over and the model slowly descends to the floor of the valley. What to notice here is that <b>the speed is not uniform</b>: at the start the slope is steep, so the steps are long and progress is fast. As you approach the bottom the slope shrinks, the steps shorten and the model starts crawling.</p><p>That slowdown is not a flaw but the nature of the method: the step size is proportional to the slope, and the slope goes to zero at the bottom. It is annoying in practice though, and it is exactly why modern optimisers (Momentum, Adam) exist; you will meet them on Track 2.</p><p>The real message here: <b>there is no single clever move in learning.</b> There is the same dumb step repeated thousands of times.</p>',
  'nasil-ogrenir:7': '<p>The learning rate η is not something the model learns. <b>You choose it.</b> Settings like this are called <b>hyperparameters</b>, and much of a good result in machine learning comes from choosing them well.</p><p>If η is chosen badly one of two things happens, and both are bad.</p><p><b>Too small:</b> the direction is right but the steps are tiny. The model is on the right path, it just never arrives. In a real project that means GPU days burned for nothing.</p><p><b>Too large:</b> every step overshoots the valley onto the opposite slope, and higher up at that. The error grows every round, the numbers overflow and you see <code>loss: nan</code> on your screen. Instead of learning, the model moves away.</p><p>The healthy range in between is not wide and it varies by problem. This is why η is the first thing tuned before training starts.</p>',
  'nasil-ogrenir:8': '<p>Everything you learned comes together in one line here, and the algorithm will actually run.</p><p>Three things to watch. <b>The sign:</b> the gradient points uphill, so it has to be a minus; make it a plus and the model climbs the hill (gradient ascent) and the error grows. <b>The multiplication:</b> the step size is the learning rate times the slope; divide instead and the step scales inversely and the model blows up. <b>The order:</b> the slope is computed first, then the parameter is updated.</p><p>It will run even if you write it wrong, and you will see the result. The fastest way to learn is to see with your own eyes what the wrong answer produces.</p>',

  'ezberleme:1': '<p>The previous lesson set a rule: test a model on data it has not seen. This lesson makes that rule workable.</p><p>The method is simple and non-negotiable: split the data in two from the start. The <b>training set</b> is the part the model sees in order to learn. The <b>test set</b> stays locked; the model never sees it during training.</p><p>Why split at the very beginning? Because once a model has seen a piece of data, that data stops being an exam. Show a student the exam question in advance and the exam is void; the same applies here.</p><p>In this lesson the model will see 10 of the 14 points and never see the other 4. All the measurement happens on those 4.</p>',
  'ezberleme:2': '<p>You also choose how flexible the model will be. Here the flexibility is set by the <b>polynomial degree</b>.</p><p>Degree 1 is a line: it can only rotate and shift. Degree 2 is a parabola and can bend once. As the degree grows the curve can make more bends, that is get closer to the points.</p><p>At the extreme this happens: with a high enough degree the curve <b>passes exactly through every training point</b>. The training error goes to zero. That looks like a success.</p><p>The one thing to understand here: <b>the training error always falls as model complexity rises.</b> That is not an observation but a mathematical necessity; whatever a less flexible model can do, a more flexible one can already do. So the training error cannot be a measure of quality.</p>',
  'ezberleme:3': '<p>Now we look at the points we kept hidden. The model never saw them, so this is a real exam.</p><p>What you will see: while the training error falls, the test error <b>starts to rise</b> past a certain point. That is, the more flexible the model becomes the worse it predicts.</p><p>The reason is that the data contains two things at once. There is a <b>rule</b> (the thing we are after) and there is <b>noise</b> (measurement error, chance, individual variation). A flexible model cannot tell them apart; to sit exactly on the points it learns the noise as well. And noise by definition does not repeat: in new data it is completely different.</p><p>This is called <b>overfitting</b>, and the only way to catch it is to measure on data the model has not seen.</p>',
  'ezberleme:4': '<p>Put the two curves side by side and you get the most recognisable plot in machine learning.</p><p>The <b>training error</b> falls monotonically with complexity. The <b>test error</b> falls first, passes through a minimum, then rises. A <b>U</b> appears, and what you are looking for is the bottom of that U.</p><p>The region on the left is <b>underfitting</b>: the model is not flexible enough to catch the rule and is bad in both training and testing. The region on the right is <b>overfitting</b>: the model learned the noise too, and is perfect in training and bad in testing. The balance between them is the most fundamental trade-off in the field, and on Track 1 it will be decomposed numerically under the name <b>bias-variance</b>.</p><p>The practical consequence is one sentence: "100% on the training set" is a warning sign, not something to boast about.</p>',
  'algoritma:1': '<p>An algorithm is a precise sequence of steps for doing a job. Like a recipe: what to do in what order is fixed, there is no room for interpretation, and the same input gives the same result every time.</p><p>This lesson is about sorting, but sorting is not the goal in itself. <b>You sort in order to make searching cheap.</b></p><p>Here is the difference. Search an unsorted list and all you can do is look from beginning to end; with n elements you spend n steps on average. If the list is sorted you look at the middle element, work out which half your target is in, and eliminate the other half in one move. Every look halves the search space, so <b>log₂n</b> steps are enough.</p><p>That is not "a bit faster". On a billion records it is the difference between a billion steps and thirty. This is why the choice of algorithm is usually more decisive than the hardware.</p>',
  'algoritma:2': '<p>You can sort eight shuffled boxes at a glance. A computer cannot, because <b>it does not see them all at once.</b></p><p>A computer has only two moves: <b>compare</b> two elements and <b>swap</b> two elements. It can do nothing else. There is no instruction saying "put the big one at the end"; even finding the big one takes a series of comparisons.</p><p>So "sort" is not an algorithm, it is a <b>wish</b>. An algorithm is the thing that says, step by step, how to fulfil that wish using only compare and swap.</p><p>That is the essence of programming: translating what a human does at a glance into the primitive moves a machine can actually make.</p>',
  'algoritma:3': '<p>Bubble sort repeats a single rule, and that rule is one sentence: <b>look at a neighbouring pair, swap them if they are out of order, move one to the right.</b></p><p>When you reach the end of the list a <b>pass</b> is complete. Then you start again from the beginning. That is all.</p><p>Watch for this: at no point does the algorithm see the whole list. At any moment it looks at just two neighbours. And yet it produces the right result. In the next step you will see that this is not a coincidence but a necessity.</p><p>The name comes from that: on every pass the large numbers <b>bubble</b> towards the end like bubbles rising to the surface.</p>',
  'algoritma:4': '<p>To "understand" an algorithm is not to memorise its steps. It is <b>to be able to show why it produces the right answer.</b></p><p>Bubble sort\'s proof fits in one sentence: during a pass the largest remaining number is carried one step right at every comparison, because it is larger than every element it meets. So <b>at the end of every pass the largest remaining number is definitely in place.</b></p><p>From that it follows directly: after k passes the last k elements are certainly correct, and after n passes the whole list is sorted. That is not an observation but a guarantee; it holds for any list you give it.</p><p>The cost follows from the same reasoning. Every pass scans the whole list and there are n passes, so the number of comparisons grows roughly with n². That fact matters later: n² growth is acceptable at a thousand elements and not at a million.</p>',
  'algoritma:5': '<p>Everything you have seen so far was a <b>classical algorithm</b>: a human writes the rule and the computer applies it. The rule is fixed, provable, and gives the same answer to the same input every time.</p><p>Machine learning differs in exactly one place: <b>the human does not write the rule.</b> The human supplies examples and a learning procedure, and the computer extracts the rule from the data itself.</p><p>That change has a price. In a classical algorithm you can show line by line why the output is what it is; in machine learning you cannot, because the rule consists of numbers nobody wrote. And the output is probabilistic rather than certain.</p><p>What you get in return is this: <b>you can solve problems whose rule you cannot write.</b> You cannot spell out "is there a cat in this photo" step by step, but you can show tens of thousands of examples. The rest of the course is built on that trade.</p>',

  'siniflandirma:1': '<p>So far the model predicted a <b>number</b>: an exam score, a house price. That is called <b>regression</b>. Now we will predict a <b>category</b>: pass or fail, spam or not. That is called <b>classification</b>.</p><p>The difference is not just the type of the output; the <b>geometry</b> of the job changes too. In regression the model looks for a curve that runs <b>through</b> the points. In classification it looks for a line that <b>separates</b> them.</p><p>That separating line is called the <b>decision boundary</b>. The model assigns a label by looking at which side of the boundary a point falls on. So classification is essentially the job of <b>dividing space</b>.</p><p>Different models divide it differently: logistic regression draws a straight boundary, decision trees make staircases perpendicular to the axes, neural networks can draw any curve they like. Tracks 1 and 2 are largely lessons about those differences.</p>',
  'siniflandirma:2': '<p>A model does not really say "spam" directly. It first produces a <b>probability</b>: "this is 83% likely to be spam". The label comes from applying a <b>threshold</b> to that probability, usually 0.5.</p><p>Where the probability comes from matters: from the <b>distance to the decision boundary</b>. For a point sitting right on the boundary the model is undecided and the probability is near 0.5. The further from the boundary, the more confident the model and the closer the probability to the extremes.</p><p>The real lesson here: <b>the threshold is not part of the model, it is your decision.</b> You can move it without changing the model at all and the behaviour changes completely.</p><p>And it is not a technical setting but a <b>cost decision</b>. Lower the threshold and you catch more but raise more false alarms. Which is more expensive depends on the problem: in a spam filter throwing away a legitimate email is severe, in cancer screening missing a patient is severe. The next lesson is about measuring exactly that.</p>',

  'metrikler:1': '<p>Accuracy is the most intuitive measure: the share of correct predictions among all predictions. And for exactly that reason it is the most misleading one.</p><p>The problem appears with <b>imbalanced data</b>: fraud, a rare disease, a manufacturing defect. In these problems the thing you are looking for is a tiny minority of the data.</p><p>Say 97% of transactions are clean. A program that learns nothing and calls every transaction "clean" gets 97% accuracy. That number looks good, but the program has not caught a single fraud. So <b>accuracy here measures the base rate, not the model.</b></p><p>A practical rule: when you see an accuracy figure the first thing to ask is not how good the model is but <b>what the class distribution is</b>. Accuracy cannot be read without knowing the distribution.</p>',
  'metrikler:2': '<p>Accuracy throws every error into the same bag. But there are two different errors and their costs differ greatly.</p><p><b>A false positive:</b> the model said "fraud" and it was not. An innocent customer was blocked. <b>A false negative:</b> the model said "clean" and it was fraud. You missed it.</p><p>Two measures separate these. <b>Precision</b>: of the things the model called positive, how many really were? That is, how many of your alarms are real. <b>Recall</b>: of the real positives, how many did you manage to catch?</p><p>The critical point: these two <b>work against each other</b>. Lower the threshold and you catch more (recall rises) but raise more false alarms (precision falls). You cannot raise both at once; you can only move the whole trade-off to a better place by genuinely improving the model.</p><p>Which one to prioritise is not a technical question: it depends on which error costs more.</p>',
  'metrikler:3': '<p>Precision and recall depend on a single <b>threshold</b>. Change the threshold and both change. So how good is the model itself, independently of the threshold?</p><p>The <b>ROC curve</b> answers that. It walks through every possible threshold and marks two numbers for each: the share caught and the false alarm rate. A curve appears, and that curve looks at the model\'s <b>discriminative power</b> rather than at one threshold.</p><p>The <b>AUC</b> is the area under that curve. Its intuitive meaning: pick a random positive and a random negative example, and the AUC is the probability that the model gives the positive one the higher score. 0.5 is a coin flip and 1.0 is perfect separation.</p><p>With one warning: because the AUC collapses everything into a single number it <b>hides which region you will operate in</b>. On imbalanced data the PR curve gives a more honest picture. And whether a small gap between two AUCs is real is decided by a statistical test, not by eye.</p>',
  'bolme:1': '<p>The previous lesson split the data in two: training and test. In a real project that is not enough, because there is one more job in between.</p><p>When you train a model you choose not only its parameters but also its <b>settings</b>: the polynomial degree, the learning rate, the tree depth. You have to try those settings somewhere. Try them on the test set and the test set stops being an honest measurement.</p><p>So the data is split in three and each part has exactly one job:</p><p><b>Training</b> → learn. The model extracts its parameters from this data.<br><b>Validation</b> → choose. You compare settings and models here.<br><b>Test</b> → measure. Only at the very end, once.</p><p>The rule is strict: <b>the more times you looked at the test set, the more optimistic your result is.</b> The moment you look at it and change your model, the test set quietly turns into a validation set and loses its honesty.</p>',
  'bolme:2': '<p>A single split has one weakness: <b>it depends on luck.</b> With little data, which examples land in the test set can change the result considerably. If the easy examples land there the model looks better than it is; if the hard ones do, worse.</p><p><b>k-fold cross validation</b> removes that. The data is split into k parts. On every round one part is the test and the rest is training. After k rounds every example has been tested exactly once.</p><p>The gain is not only a fairer average: instead of a single number you get <b>a distribution of k numbers</b>. So you also see how volatile the result is. Only with that distribution can you argue about whether the gap between two models is real.</p><p>One warning: random splitting is not right for every dataset. In a <b>time series</b> you end up seeing the future and predicting the past. In <b>grouped data</b> (several records of the same patient, say) if the same group lands in both training and test, the model recognises the person rather than the rule. Both are leakage.</p>',

  'sizinti:1': '<p>Data leakage is the most expensive and the quietest mistake in machine learning. The model looks wonderful and collapses in production.</p><p>Its definition is one sentence: <b>giving the model information that will not be available at prediction time.</b></p><p>Why is it hard to catch? Because its symptom is not a bad result but <b>a very good one</b>. The model has already seen the answer by an indirect route. When the accuracy comes out higher than you expected, the right reaction is suspicion rather than delight.</p><p>The three most common forms: <b>columns that are filled in after the event</b> (was the transaction refunded, was the case sent for manual review), <b>features derived from the target</b>, and <b>preprocessing fitted on all the data</b> (fitting the scaler before splitting leaks the test set\'s mean into the model).</p>',
  'sizinti:2': '<p>The only reliable test for leakage is not technical but <b>temporal</b>. Ask one question about every feature:</p><p><b>"Will this information be in my hands at the moment I make the prediction?"</b></p><p>If the answer is no it is leakage. If the answer is "I am not sure" it counts as leakage too; uncertainty does not get the benefit of the doubt.</p><p>The column name sometimes gives a clue but is never enough. The real question is <b>when that value was written</b>. A column with the same name may be filled before the prediction in one system and after it in another. You only find that out by asking the team that produces the data.</p><p>And remember the trade: when you drop a leaking feature your accuracy falls. That fall is not a loss but <b>a return to reality</b>. The cost of a model that collapses in production is far higher than those few points.</p>',

  'mat-matris:1': '<p>Learning a matrix as a table of numbers is like knowing the chess pieces without knowing the game. <b>A matrix is an operation:</b> a rule that moves every point in space to another point.</p><p>The four numbers of a 2×2 matrix say what to do to the plane: rotate, stretch, shear. Put a shape through that transformation and it moves and changes form.</p><p>The <b>determinant</b> is the one-number summary of that operation: <b>by what factor it multiplies the area.</b> That is not a metaphor but a measurable equality; divide the new area by the old one and you get the absolute value of the determinant.</p><p>Its sign carries information too: a negative determinant means the transformation flipped the shape over like a mirror.</p>',
  'mat-matris:2': '<p>Something special happens when the determinant is zero: <b>the area falls to zero.</b> The shape is crushed onto a line, or even a point.</p><p>What that means is that the transformation <b>destroyed a dimension</b>. Different inputs land on the same output. And once they have, you cannot say which one they came from, because the information has been erased irrecoverably.</p><p>The algebraic counterpart: if the determinant is zero <b>the matrix has no inverse</b>. The undo operation is undefined.</p><p>The counterpart on the data side is very concrete: if two columns are multiples of one another (say "metres" and "centimetres") the determinant of that data matrix is zero. The model cannot tell the two columns apart and the question "how much does each contribute" has no single answer. The ridge lesson on Track 1 addresses exactly this problem.</p>',
  'mat-matris:3': '<p>Multiplying two matrices means applying two operations <b>one after the other</b>. First one, then the other.</p><p>From that it follows directly: <b>the order matters.</b> Rotating and then stretching does not give the same result as stretching and then rotating. It is the same in daily life: putting your socks on before your shoes is not the same as the reverse.</p><p>So in matrix multiplication <b>AB is generally not equal to BA</b>. The commutativity you are used to with numbers does not hold here, and that is not a flaw but the nature of the operation.</p><p>The determinants, on the other hand, do multiply: det(AB) = det(A)·det(B). So the order changes the result but <b>not by what factor the area grows</b>; two stretches produce the same total growth whichever order they are applied in.</p>',
  'mat-matris:4': '<p>Now the link to AI. <b>A neural network layer is, by definition, a matrix multiplication.</b></p><p>In a layer every neuron multiplies all the inputs by its own weights and sums them. Instead of writing that out for every neuron, you put the weights in a matrix and the whole layer becomes a single multiplication.</p><p>That has two consequences. First, the mathematics of deep learning is really linear algebra. Second and more practical: all the multiplications inside a matrix product are <b>independent of each other</b>, so they can be done at the same time.</p><p>This is why GPUs made AI possible. A GPU is not fast at one operation; it does thousands of independent operations at once. And a matrix multiplication is exactly that kind of job.</p>',

  'mat-olasilik:1': '<p>The sentence "the probability of this coin landing tails is 0.5" says nothing about a single toss. It makes no promise; it states <b>a long run rate</b>.</p><p>In a small number of trials the observed rate can deviate noticeably from the true probability. As the number of trials grows the deviation shrinks, and that shrinking has a speed: the measurement error falls roughly with <b>1/√N</b>.</p><p>The practical meaning of that formula is annoying: to <b>halve</b> the error you have to <b>quadruple</b> the number of samples. To cut it to a tenth, multiply by a hundred.</p><p>This is why numbers from small test sets cannot be trusted, and why model comparisons come with confidence intervals. The same rule will be the answer to "how many examples are enough" in the evaluation lesson on Track 4.</p>',
  'mat-olasilik:2': '<p>A test being good does <b>not on its own determine</b> what its result means. This is the statistical fact that runs most against intuition.</p><p>The quality of a test is measured with two numbers. <b>Sensitivity</b>: what share of the ill it calls positive. <b>Specificity</b>: what share of the healthy it correctly calls negative. Say both are 99%, so a very good test.</p><p>Now the real question: your test came out positive, what is the probability that you are actually ill?</p><p>The answer depends not on the quality of the test but also on <b>how common the disease is</b>. If the disease is rare there are so many healthy people that the false positives coming from a small percentage of them swamp the number of genuinely ill ones. The result: most of those testing positive are actually healthy.</p><p>That calculation is in front of you in this step. As you look at the numbers, watch this: the test never changes, only the world does.</p>',
  'mat-olasilik:3': '<p>The counterpart of the same calculation in machine learning is exact; only the names change.</p><p>The test\'s <b>sensitivity</b> = the model\'s <b>recall</b>. The test\'s <b>specificity</b> = one minus the false alarm rate. And the question "it came out positive, is it really ill" is <b>precision</b>.</p><p>What follows matters: <b>precision is not a property of the model.</b> It is a joint property of the model and the world. Move the same model to an environment where the event is rarer and its precision falls, even though you changed nothing.</p><p>The practical consequence: a model\'s precision figure from one setting is not valid in another. If the base rate changed, the number changed too.</p>',
  'mat-olasilik:4': '<p>When the base rate is low the design itself changes. Intuition misleads here, because everybody tries to raise the sensitivity first.</p><p>But with a rare event the real bottleneck is not sensitivity but <b>specificity</b>. The reason is numerical: there are very many negative examples, so even a small improvement in the false alarm rate cuts the total number of alarms greatly. Raising the sensitivity, on the other hand, can only pay off over the already small number of positives.</p><p>The extreme case: even if you take the sensitivity to 100%, that is even if you miss no case at all, the precision can stay low because of the false alarms.</p><p>This is why two moves are standard on rare event problems: raise the threshold and improve the model in the direction of specificity. The numbers in this step measure the return on both routes side by side.</p>',
  'neden-simdi:1': '<p>The standard answer to "why did AI take off now?" is three words: data, compute, algorithms. It is true but empty, because it does not say how much each contributed.</p><p>This lesson makes that question <b>measurable</b>. The setup is an experiment: one problem, a fixed test set and three separate levers.</p><p><b>Data:</b> how many examples we train on. <b>Compute:</b> how many rounds we train for. <b>Algorithm:</b> which model family we use.</p><p>First a <b>baseline</b> is established: a raw model, few examples, few rounds. Every number from here on is read against that baseline. And the rule of the experiment: <b>only one thing</b> changes at a time, so it is never unclear what caused a difference.</p>',
  'neden-simdi:2': '<p>Now we pull the levers one at a time. Only one grows while the other two stay fixed.</p><p>The expectation would be that raising the data a lot should lower the error noticeably. What you see in this step does not match that expectation, and the reason matters.</p><p><b>A linear model can only ever draw a line, however much data you give it.</b> If the relationship you are after is a curve, extra data does not help you catch it; it just pins down the same wrong line more precisely. On Track 1 we will call this <b>bias</b>: the error that comes from the model\'s own constraint and does not close with data.</p><p>The same logic holds for compute. Training a converged model for more rounds gains nothing; it has already gone as far as it can.</p><p>The conclusion: <b>each lever pays off only as far as the state of the others allows.</b></p>',
  'neden-simdi:3': '<p>Now we pull all three at once. The number here is neither the sum nor the product of the individual gains; it is larger than both.</p><p>The reason: the levers <b>unlock each other</b>. A flexible model can only use its capacity with enough data, otherwise it memorises. And enough data can only be processed with enough training rounds. Without all three at once, none of them shows its potential.</p><p>That is why "which one matters most" is the wrong question. The levers are not independent; <b>the effect of one depends on the state of the others.</b></p><p>It is also why AI took off in the last decade: large datasets, GPUs and architectures like the transformer became available <b>in the same period</b>. Had one been missing, the other two could not have produced today\'s result.</p>',
  'neden-simdi:4': '<p>The most important part of an experiment is saying what it does <b>not</b> show.</p><p>This experiment was run on one problem, at a small scale, with a chosen model family. The ratios that come out of it <b>belong to this setup</b>; another problem gives other numbers. What it demonstrates is not the numbers themselves but the <b>mechanism</b>: the levers unlock each other.</p><p>A second limit: no lever pays off forever. Past a certain point neither more data nor more rounds gains anything; the noise floor has been reached. You will see that numerically in the bias-variance lesson on Track 1 and in the scaling laws on Track 3.</p><p>And a caveat: the "algorithm" lever here was an architectural change. In real life an algorithmic improvement is usually harder and does not always give such a clean gain.</p>',

  'arama-uzayi:1': '<p>There is a standard way of explaining a puzzle to a computer: writing it as <b>states</b> and <b>transitions</b>.</p><p>A <b>state</b> is the complete snapshot of the world at that moment. In the water jug puzzle a state is the pair "how many litres in jug A, how many in jug B". A <b>transition</b> is an allowed move that takes you from one state to another: fill, empty, pour from one into the other.</p><p>With that writing the problem turns into a <b>graph</b>: the nodes are states and the edges are moves. And solving the puzzle reduces to searching for a path from one node to another.</p><p>One thing to note: not all combinations of a state can actually <b>occur</b>. Some states the rules do not allow cannot be reached by any sequence of moves. So the search space is smaller than the number of combinations you would count on paper.</p>',
  'arama-uzayi:2': '<p>Once the graph is built the question is: how do we find the <b>shortest</b> path to the goal?</p><p><b>Breadth-first search</b> (BFS) guarantees it. The method: first all the states reachable from the start in one move, then those reachable in two, then three. That is, the search widens <b>layer by layer</b>.</p><p>The proof of the guarantee follows from that ordering: if you found the goal in layer d, then had a shorter path existed it would already have been seen in an earlier layer. So the first path you find is the shortest.</p><p>It has one condition: this guarantee holds <b>when every move costs the same</b>. If the moves have different costs BFS is not enough; there you need Dijkstra or A*, and you will meet them on Track 1.</p>',
  'arama-uzayi:3': '<p>One detail determines the cost of the search: <b>are you noting down the states you have seen?</b></p><p>If you do not, you reach the same state again and again by different routes and expand it every time. The search now walks a <b>tree</b> rather than a graph, and that tree grows exponentially with depth.</p><p>Keeping the seen states in a set cuts that off: the second time you see a state you skip it. The work is then bounded by the <b>number of states</b> in the space, not by how many routes lead to them.</p><p>That one extra line turns an exponential search into a finite one. And the idea is not confined to this lesson: dynamic programming, memoisation and caching are different names for the same principle. <b>Do not do the same work twice.</b></p>',
  'arama-uzayi:4': '<p>Now the limit of search algorithms. <b>No algorithm can find an answer that is not in the space.</b></p><p>In the water jug puzzle some target amounts cannot be obtained by any sequence of moves. The reason is not the search but the <b>arithmetic</b>: every move you can make amounts to adding and subtracting whole multiples of the jug capacities, so every amount you can reach has to be a multiple of the <b>greatest common divisor</b> of the two capacities.</p><p>If the target is not one of those multiples there is no solution. The search could run forever and not find it, because what it is looking for is not in the space.</p><p>The engineering lesson: when a problem cannot be solved, the first question is not "is there a better algorithm". First ask <b>whether the solution is in the search space at all</b>. That distinction saves months of wasted effort.</p>',

  'kombinatorik:1': '<p>The most misleading intuition in computer science is this: "slower" and "impossible" are taken to be on the same scale. They are not.</p><p>An algorithm\'s cost is measured by how it grows as the input grows. <b>Polynomial</b> growth (n², n³) is manageable: double the input and the work grows by a few times. <b>Exponential</b> growth (2ⁿ) is not: add <b>one</b> to the input and the work <b>doubles</b>. <b>Factorial</b> growth (n!) is worse still: add one and the work grows n times.</p><p>That difference is not one of degree but of <b>kind</b>. On small inputs all three look fast; as the input grows two stay usable and one becomes physically impossible.</p><p>The numbers in this step put all three on the same axis. You will see there that the difference is not "a bit".</p>',
  'kombinatorik:2': '<p>Now you will feel the explosion in your own browser rather than in a table.</p><p>The problem is the <b>travelling salesman</b>: find the shortest tour that visits every city once and returns to the start. The brute force solution is perfect: try every tour and pick the shortest. It guarantees the right answer.</p><p>The problem is the count. Add one city and the number of tours to try grows <b>n times</b>. So adding a few cities does not mean "it takes a bit longer", it means "it never finishes".</p><p>The reason this page stops at a certain number of cities is not a theoretical choice: add a few more and the browser really does freeze. That is how you feel the combinatorial explosion.</p>',
  'kombinatorik:3': '<p>The natural reflex: "if computers get faster the problem goes away." This step measures that reflex.</p><p>The difference is <b>where</b> the speedup goes. With polynomial growth a faster machine <b>multiplies</b> the size you can solve. With exponential growth it only <b>adds</b> a few units to it.</p><p>The reason: with exponential growth every new unit already doubles the work. However much you speed the machine up, that speedup is a fixed multiplier, and a fixed multiplier only buys a fixed number of units.</p><p>The practical consequence is clear: <b>buying hardware is not a solution to an exponential problem.</b> Waiting a few years for a faster machine comes to the same thing.</p>',
  'kombinatorik:4': '<p>Hardware cannot change the growth class. <b>An algorithm can.</b> That is what rescues you.</p><p>On the travelling salesman, brute force tries every tour. A better approach (Held-Karp) stores the intermediate results instead of recomputing them again and again: the "do not do the same work twice" idea from the previous lesson. That shrinks the exponent and noticeably grows the size you can solve.</p><p>But to be honest: <b>Held-Karp is exponential too.</b> It is a more slowly growing exponential, that is all. So it does not make the problem easy, it only pushes the wall further out.</p><p>In the real world the usual way of coping with exponential problems is this: give up on finding the exact optimum and settle for a <b>good enough</b> solution. The A* and hyperparameter search lessons on Track 1 are examples of that trade.</p>',
  'knn:1': '<p>Every model you have seen so far went through a <b>training</b> phase: it looked at the data and adjusted its parameters. k-NN does none of that.</p><p>k-NN\'s "training" amounts to putting the data into memory. No computation, no parameters, no loss function. All the work is deferred to <b>prediction time</b>: when a new question arrives it looks at all the data, finds the <b>nearest k neighbours</b> to the query point, and answers with whichever class is in the majority.</p><p>This approach is called <b>lazy learning</b>. The assumption behind it is one sentence: <b>points that are close together have the same label.</b> When that assumption holds, k-NN works surprisingly well.</p><p>The price is in the cost. Other models are expensive in training and cheap at prediction; k-NN is the reverse. Every prediction means scanning all the data, so as the data grows every question gets more expensive.</p>',
  'knn:2': '<p>k says how many neighbours to consult. And it is the model\'s <b>only setting</b>.</p><p><b>If k is small</b> the model looks at very few neighbours. The decision boundary becomes wiggly and a single mislabelled point creates an island around itself. So it is exposed to noise: this is what overfitting looks like in k-NN.</p><p><b>If k is large</b> the model averages over a wide neighbourhood. The boundary smooths out, the noise is erased, but so are the genuine details. At the extreme, when k equals the whole dataset, the model gives everyone the same answer.</p><p>So k is the k-NN name of a familiar dial: the <b>flexibility dial</b>. It does the same job as λ in ridge, the depth in a tree and C in an SVM, and the right value is chosen from the data by cross validation.</p>',

  'lojistik:1': '<p>It has "regression" in its name but its job is classification. The name comes from the first half of what it does.</p><p>The model works in three steps. <b>First:</b> exactly as in linear regression it computes a score, <code>z = w·x + b</code>. That score can be any number from minus to plus infinity. <b>Second:</b> it passes the score through a <b>sigmoid</b>, which squashes any number into the range 0 to 1, that is turns the score into a probability. <b>Third:</b> in training the loss used is <b>cross entropy</b>.</p><p>Its structure implies a constraint: because the score is linear, <b>the decision boundary is always straight</b>. On data that needs a curved boundary logistic regression falls short.</p><p>But the same constraint is the source of its strength: every feature has a coefficient and that coefficient can be read directly. You can say "when this variable rises, the probability of the outcome moves this way". In most models that sentence cannot be formed.</p>',
  'lojistik:2': '<p>Logistic regression dates from the 1950s. Why is it still used in the middle of the deep learning era?</p><p>Because <b>choosing a model is not an accuracy race.</b> In a real project there are at least four more criteria.</p><p><b>Interpretability:</b> the coefficients are readable and the decision can be justified. In fields like credit and health that is a legal requirement. <b>Calibration:</b> the probabilities it produces are close to the real rates; when it says "70%" then roughly 7 of 10 cases really are. Many flexible models do not do that. <b>Data appetite:</b> it works with few examples, because it has few parameters to learn. <b>Cost:</b> it is cheap to train and to run, and simple to maintain.</p><p>The practical consequence: on most tabular problems logistic regression is <b>the first baseline to build</b>. A more complex model earns its place only if it can be shown statistically to beat that baseline.</p>',

  'agac:1': '<p>A decision tree works by splitting the data in two over and over. At every node it asks a single question, "is this feature above this threshold?", and separates the data into two branches.</p><p>The real question is: <b>which question should it ask?</b> The tree\'s answer rests on a measurable criterion.</p><p>The number that measures how mixed a group is is called <b>impurity</b>; its most common form is <b>Gini</b>. If a group is entirely one class the Gini is zero (perfectly pure); if the classes are half and half it is at its highest (maximally mixed).</p><p>The tree tries every candidate threshold and computes: <b>the parent\'s impurity minus the weighted impurity of the children.</b> That difference is called the <b>gain</b>, and the tree picks the threshold that makes the gain largest.</p><p>One detail matters: this choice is <b>greedy</b>. The tree does not ask "will this split help me later", it just takes the best one available now. Finding the globally optimal tree is NP-hard, which is why everybody is greedy.</p>',
  'agac:2': '<p>Every split of the tree rests on a single feature and a single threshold. The geometric consequence: <b>every cut is perpendicular to an axis.</b></p><p>So a tree cannot draw a diagonal line. It can only approximate a diagonal boundary with a <b>staircase</b>: small vertical and horizontal steps.</p><p>As the depth grows the steps get finer and the approximation improves. But at no depth do you get a true diagonal; you only get a finer staircase.</p><p>That has two practical consequences. Trees are <b>insensitive to scaling</b>: change a column from metres to kilometres and the threshold scales too, leaving the tree the same. On the other hand they are <b>sensitive to rotation</b>: rotating the data changes the problem completely as far as the tree is concerned. Linear models and SVMs do not have that problem.</p>',
  'agac:3': '<p>A single tree has two fundamental weaknesses and both come from its structure.</p><p><b>Instability.</b> Because the tree is built greedily the root split is decisive; if the root changes, everything below it changes. Remove a few points from the data and retrain and a completely different tree can come out. This is called <b>high variance</b>: the model is overly sensitive to small changes in the training data.</p><p><b>The staircase artefact.</b> The decision regions have sharp corners. If the true boundary is smooth the tree can never catch it smoothly.</p><p>That weakness gave birth to the two most successful ideas in machine learning, and both rest on the same intuition: <b>do not trust one tree, use many.</b></p><p><b>Bagging</b> grows trees in parallel and independently of each other and lets them vote; it lowers variance. <b>Boosting</b> grows trees in sequence, each targeting the previous one\'s error; it lowers bias. The next two lessons are those.</p>',

  'soft-split:1': '<p>This lesson follows one question: why can decision trees not be trained with gradient descent?</p><p>The answer is hidden in the tree\'s smallest piece. Every node asks a single question: "is x greater than the threshold t?" If yes the example goes to the right branch, if no to the left. There is nothing in between; an example 0.01 to the left of the threshold goes entirely left and one 0.01 to the right goes entirely right.</p><p>Mathematically that is a <b>step function</b>. And the derivative of a step function is <b>zero everywhere</b> (and undefined at the threshold).</p><p>Recall gradient descent\'s only question: "which way should I move the parameter?" It takes the answer from the derivative. Here the answer it gets is: <i>no direction, it makes no difference.</i></p><p>This is why classical trees are built by greedy search rather than gradient descent. The consequence: trees are interpretable but <b>not end to end learnable</b>; they cannot be plugged into the same pipeline as neural networks.</p>',
  'soft-split:2': '<p>The idea that solves it amounts to a single change: <b>replace the step with a sigmoid.</b></p><p>The gate no longer gives a sharp yes/no but a <b>weight</b> between 0 and 1. Every example goes to both branches, just with different weights.</p><p>The sharpness of the sigmoid is set by the <b>temperature</b> (T) and it works like a dial. <b>As T approaches zero</b> the sigmoid turns into a step; you get the classical decision tree back, with maximum interpretability. <b>When T is large</b> the gate softens right out; the model becomes more flexible, more like a neural network.</p><p>The real gain here is not having to choose. It is not a pick between a classical tree and a neural network but <b>a continuous dial on which you can stop at any point in between</b>.</p>',
  'soft-split:3': '<p>Softening is not an aesthetic preference; it has a mathematical consequence.</p><p>A sigmoid is <b>differentiable</b> and its derivative is never exactly zero. It is largest near the threshold, where the decision is most delicate, and shrinks as you move away without ever vanishing.</p><p>What that means: the question "should I move the threshold left or right?" now has an answer. That is, <b>the tree became trainable by backpropagation.</b></p><p>And this is not only about the threshold. Which feature to look at, the leaf values, the temperature: all of it can be learned at the same time. Instead of being built greedily node by node, the whole tree is <b>optimised end to end</b>.</p>',
  'soft-split:4': '<p>Now the concrete consequence of the difference. The same tree structure, the same leaf values; the only difference is whether the gate is hard or soft.</p><p><b>In a hard tree</b> the example goes to a single leaf and the prediction is that leaf\'s value. No value in between can be produced. Crossing from one side of the threshold to the other, the prediction <b>jumps</b>.</p><p><b>In a soft tree</b> both branches flow with a weight and the prediction is the <b>weighted average</b> of the leaves. Around the threshold the transition is smooth.</p><p>This is not an academic detail. In fields like credit scoring "customers at the threshold" is a known problem: a very small difference in the input creates a large jump in the decision, and that is hard to explain to a customer. A soft gate removes exactly that jump.</p>',
  'soft-split:5': '<p>The step where you prove you understood: you will complete the code for the soft gate and run it.</p><p>The gate has to satisfy three things. <b>The output has to be a weight</b>, that is between 0 and 1; this is why a sigmoid is needed and ReLU will not do, because it is unbounded. <b>The threshold has to be subtracted</b>, not added; the expression x − t is what makes the gate turn at t. <b>The temperature has to be the divisor</b>; dividing by T sets the sharpness of the slope, while multiplying by it or dividing by x makes the gate meaningless.</p><p>It will run even if you write it wrong and you will see the result. These three lines are the core of a real library (neural-trees).</p>',
  'soft-tree:1': '<p>You saw the idea in the previous lesson. This one actually trains it and puts it side by side with a classical tree.</p><p>To see where the difference comes from, look at the gate. CART\'s gate looks at a single feature: "is x > t?" That is why its cuts are perpendicular to the axes. A soft tree\'s gate looks at <b>a weighted sum of all the features</b>: σ((w₁x + w₂y + b)/T). That is a linear expression, and a linear expression <b>can cut diagonally</b>.</p><p>So the gain does not come from "softness" alone. The gate being linear removes CART\'s staircase constraint entirely.</p><p>In this step you will see the boundaries and the parameter counts of the two models on the same data. What to watch is not only the accuracy but <b>with how many parameters</b> it was reached.</p>',
  'soft-tree:2': '<p>One end of the temperature dial is not free, and this step measures the price.</p><p>As T shrinks the model gets sharper, that is more interpretable. But at the same time it becomes untrainable. The reason is mathematical.</p><p>The derivative of a sigmoid is proportional to σ(1−σ). As T shrinks, σ <b>saturates</b> at 0 or 1; once saturated, σ(1−σ) goes to zero and <b>the gradient vanishes</b>. The 1/T factor in the formula tries to compensate, but the saturation grows faster.</p><p>So the sentence "T → 0 brings the classical tree back" has a sequel: it brings back the classical tree\'s <b>untrainability</b> too. The dial is continuous and there is a real price at both ends.</p><p>The practical fix is called <b>annealing</b>: start with a high T so the gradients flow and a roughly correct gate is found, then lower T step by step to sharpen it. The same idea is used in knowledge distillation and in methods like Gumbel-Softmax.</p>',
  'soft-tree:3': '<p>The circle closes in this step. You have two numbers and there appears to be a gap between them. The question: <b>is that gap real?</b></p><p>Recall the lesson from Track 0. These numbers came from a single training run and were measured on all the data; that is a <i>training accuracy</i>. It is not enough for a claim of superiority.</p><p>The protocol for an honest comparison: split the data with <b>5×2cv</b>, train both models on every fold with the same split, collect the 10 paired differences and apply the <b>Alpaydın 5×2cv F-test</b>. If p is small the difference is significant; if not, the correct sentence is "could not be shown".</p><p>And the real lesson here: finding a pretty visual, a model that runs and a high number is easy. What is hard is being able to show that the number is <b>real</b>. That trio, <b>build, compare, prove</b>, repeats with every model from here on.</p>',

  'orman:1': '<p>The previous lesson concluded that a single tree is interpretable but unstable. <b>Bagging</b> fixes that instability, and the idea comes from statistics.</p><p>The average of independent measurements is less volatile than a single measurement. The same holds for models: average models that make independent errors and the errors partly cancel out. That is, <b>averaging lowers variance.</b></p><p>The critical word is <b>independent</b>. Run the same algorithm on the same data a hundred times and you get a hundred identical trees, and averaging gains nothing. The trees have to be made different.</p><p>Random Forest does that with two kinds of randomness. <b>Bootstrap:</b> every tree is trained on a random sample of the data drawn with replacement. <b>Random features:</b> only a subset of the features is tried at every split.</p><p>One consequence matters: adding trees <b>does not cause overfitting</b>. The number of trees lowers variance, it does not raise capacity. The risk of overfitting comes from the <b>depth</b> of the trees, not their number.</p>',

  'boosting:1': '<p>Random Forest grew its trees in parallel and independently. Boosting does the opposite: it grows them <b>in sequence</b>, and every tree targets the error the previous ones left behind.</p><p>The loop does one thing per round. First the <b>residual</b> is computed: the truth minus the current prediction. Then <b>a new stump</b> (a very shallow tree) is fitted to that residual. Then the stump\'s output is added to the current prediction with a small multiplier. And again.</p><p>That small multiplier is the <b>learning rate</b> and it is critical. It is kept small in order to stop each stump from sitting exactly on the residual; if it does, it learns the noise too.</p><p>What is striking: <b>no stump is any good on its own.</b> Each one is just a threshold and two constants. But stack them and the error melts away fast. That is the whole of boosting.</p><p>There is a price: boosting will certainly memorise if it does not stop. This is why <b>boosting is never used without early stopping.</b></p>',
  'boosting:2': '<p>Both methods use "many trees" but they solve <b>completely different problems</b>.</p><p><b>Random Forest is parallel.</b> The trees are independent and can be trained at once. Each tree is deep, that is individually low bias and high variance. Averaging lowers <b>variance</b>. Adding a tree never hurts.</p><p><b>Boosting is sequential.</b> The trees depend on each other and must be trained in order. Each tree is shallow, that is a "weak learner" with high bias and low variance. Correcting errors on top of each other lowers <b>bias</b>. Past a point, adding a tree hurts.</p><p>In practice on tabular data the boosting family (XGBoost, LightGBM, CatBoost) usually beats Random Forest. But RF is one of the rare models that gives a reasonable result <i>with no tuning at all</i>. So the standard order is: build a baseline with RF first, then try to beat it with boosting, and test the difference statistically.</p>',

  'svm:1': '<p>In the classification lesson you found a line. But there are <b>infinitely many</b> lines separating two classes. Which one is best?</p><p>The SVM\'s answer is simple and strong: <b>the one that stays furthest from both classes.</b> That gap between the two classes is called the <b>margin</b>, and an SVM picks the line that makes it widest.</p><p>The intuition: if the margin is wide, a new point can drift a little either way without changing class. So a wide margin is safer generalisation.</p><p>The elegant part of the solution is this: only the points sitting on or inside the margin determine the boundary. They are called the <b>support vectors</b>. Delete every other point and the model does not change at all. The practical consequence: a million rows of data can produce an extremely compact model with a few hundred support vectors.</p><p><b>C</b> is the penalty given to error: a small C means a wide margin with tolerance for mistakes, a large C a narrow margin with a risk of overfitting. So C is the SVM name of the other flexibility dials you have seen on this track.</p>',
  'svm:2': '<p>The boundary an SVM draws is straight. So what if the data cannot be separated by a straight line?</p><p>The idea: carry the data into <b>a higher dimension</b> and it may become linearly separable. With one class in the middle and the other at both ends no single point can separate them; but invent a second dimension x² and a line is enough.</p><p>The problem: in real problems the dimension needed can be very high, even infinite. Carrying the data there is impossible.</p><p>The <b>kernel trick</b> comes in here. The SVM\'s optimisation needs the data points only through their <b>inner products</b>. And some functions can compute the value of the inner product in the high dimension <b>without ever going there</b>. So the high dimension is never actually built.</p><p>The price is not in the dimension but in the <b>number of examples</b>: because the kernel matrix is n×n the cost grows fast with the size of the data. This is why an SVM stops being practical on big data, and tree ensembles and deep learning take over there.</p>',

  'kumeleme:1': '<p>In every lesson so far there was a <b>right answer</b>: a y for every x. That is called <b>supervised learning</b>. In this lesson there are no labels.</p><p>All you have is points, and the question is: are there groups in the data, and if so which? That is called <b>unsupervised learning</b>, and k-means is its best known example.</p><p>The algorithm is surprisingly simple; it repeats two steps. <b>Assign:</b> attach every point to its nearest centre. <b>Update:</b> move every centre to the exact middle of the points attached to it. Then assign again, update again.</p><p>The algorithm stops when the centres stop moving. No teacher, no labels, not even a loss function in the classical sense; and yet if the data really is grouped, k-means finds those groups.</p><p>This family is heavily used in practice: customer segmentation, anomaly detection, document grouping.</p>',
  'kumeleme:2': '<p>k-means has no guarantee, and this step shows exactly that.</p><p>The algorithm starts by placing the centres somewhere. Where it places them determines the result. If the start is bad the algorithm can get stuck in a <b>local optimum</b>: by its own criterion it converged, stopped and returned a result; but the groups it found are wrong.</p><p>The crucial part: <b>the algorithm does not know that this is wrong.</b> In supervised learning the test set tells you "you got it wrong". Here there is nobody to say it, because there is no such thing as a right answer.</p><p>That is the general price of unsupervised learning, and in practice it is managed in three ways: running the algorithm <b>with many different starts</b> and taking the best (<code>n_init</code> in scikit-learn), using the <b>k-means++</b> initialisation that deliberately places centres far apart, and testing the result against domain knowledge.</p><p>Keep this in mind: <b>an algorithm saying "I converged" does not mean "I found the right answer".</b></p>',

  'boyut:1': '<p>Is a dataset with twenty columns really twenty dimensional? Usually not. If the columns are related to each other, the information is really carried in far fewer directions.</p><p><b>PCA</b> finds those directions. It asks one question: <b>in which direction does the data spread most?</b> That direction is the first principal component (PC1). Then it looks for the most spread direction among those perpendicular to PC1, and so on.</p><p>The mathematical counterpart: the components are the <b>eigenvectors of the covariance matrix</b> and each one\'s <b>eigenvalue</b> is the variance in that direction. The components are sorted from largest to smallest.</p><p>Two properties matter. The components are <b>perpendicular</b> to each other, so PCA rotates the data; the new axes still form a perpendicular coordinate system. And the components are <b>mixtures</b> of the original features, which is why they are considered "uninterpretable": they buy speed and noise reduction and cost interpretability.</p>',
  'boyut:2': '<p>The answer to "how many dimensions should I go down to?" is hidden in the eigenvalues: the share of variance carried by each component is known.</p><p>Three criteria are used in practice. <b>A variance threshold:</b> take as many components as it takes to keep a given share (say 95%) of the total variance. <b>The elbow:</b> pick the point where the eigenvalue curve bends and starts to flatten. <b>Downstream performance:</b> see which number of components the actual model works best with.</p><p>There are two critical warnings. <b>PCA is unsupervised:</b> it never looks at the label. It can easily discard a direction with small variance that is critical for separating the classes. The supervised alternative is LDA, and you will meet it later.</p><p><b>PCA is fitted on the training set only.</b> Fit it on all the data and split afterwards and the test set\'s information leaks into the model; that is exactly the trap from the leakage lesson. The fix is to use a <code>Pipeline</code>.</p><p>And because PCA looks at variance it is meaningless without scaling: a column with large units captures all the components on its own.</p>',
  'boyut:3': '<p>t-SNE and UMAP are often put in the same box as PCA but they do <b>a completely different job</b>.</p><p>PCA is <b>linear</b>: it rotates the data and cuts. That makes it fast, deterministic and invertible; you can unpack the data you compressed.</p><p>The aim of t-SNE and UMAP is not to preserve variance but to preserve <b>neighbourhood relations</b>: points close together in high dimensions should stay close in two. The price is heavy: they are not deterministic (a different picture on every run), not invertible, and most importantly <b>the distances between clusters and the sizes of clusters are meaningless</b>.</p><p>The most common mistake is using that output as model input. These are <b>visualisation tools</b>, not feature extractors.</p><p>The right usage: inspecting an embedding space by eye, checking whether clusters really separate, hunting for label errors. And the only reliable thing you can read off a UMAP plot is this: <b>does it separate or not.</b></p>',
  'regresyon:1': '<p>On Track 0 we found the line with gradient descent: step by step, over thousands of repetitions. There was actually no need for that at all.</p><p>The squared error is a <b>smooth, single-bottomed</b> function of the parameters. At the minimum of such a function the derivative is zero. Set the derivative to zero and solve, and you get the answer directly; no search needed.</p><p>The result is called the <b>normal equation</b>: <code>w = (XᵀX)⁻¹Xᵀy</code>. One step, no setting like a learning rate, and it gives the exact solution.</p><p>So why is gradient descent still used? Because the XᵀX matrix in the formula is p×p (p = the number of features) and <b>inverting it grows with p³</b>. At a few thousand features that computation gets heavy; at a million it is impossible. Gradient descent, by contrast, scans the data once per step and never inverts a matrix.</p>',
  'regresyon:2': '<p>The closed form always exists but is not always <b>meaningful</b>.</p><p>As two features approach being copies of one another, the XᵀX matrix starts to become <b>singular</b>: its determinant falls to zero and inverting it turns into a division by zero.</p><p>The number that measures how bad this is is called the <b>condition number</b>. Its meaning: by what factor a small change in the input grows in the output. If the condition number is large the solution is numerically unreliable.</p><p>The practical counterpart: two datasets drawn from the same process give very different coefficients. The model is unstable. This is exactly the numerical meaning of the sentence "correlated features make unpenalised regression unstable" from the ridge lesson.</p>',
  'regresyon:3': '<p>The fix is surprisingly simple: add a small number to the diagonal of the matrix. <code>w = (XᵀX + λI)⁻¹Xᵀy</code>.</p><p>That addition stops the matrix being singular and lowers the condition number; the solution becomes numerically stable.</p><p>The nice part: this formula <b>is ridge regression itself</b>. So ridge does two jobs at once. Statistically it lowers the variance, numerically it produces an invertible matrix. Two points of view, one formula.</p><p>One consequence: ridge works <b>even when there are more features than examples</b>. In that case XᵀX is certainly singular, but XᵀX + λI is not.</p><p>A practical note: real libraries never actually invert the matrix when computing this; they use a QR or SVD decomposition, because those are more stable on ill-conditioned matrices.</p>',
  'regresyon:4': '<p>There are two solution routes and the choice depends on the problem.</p><p><b>The normal equation:</b> one step, no tuning, an exact solution. But building XᵀX and inverting it gets expensive fast with the number of features. With few features it is the perfect choice.</p><p><b>Gradient descent:</b> every step scales linearly with the number of examples and features, it does not have to hold all the data in memory (it works with mini batches), and on sparse data it only visits the non-zero entries. It also works unchanged on non-linear models. The price is choosing a learning rate and watching convergence.</p><p>The threshold is roughly a few thousand features. And correlated features cause trouble on both routes: as slow convergence in gradient descent and as a bad condition number in the normal equation. The cure for both is the same: a penalty term.</p>',

  'ridge:1': '<p>In real data the columns are not independent of each other. "Monthly income" and "annual income", "floor area" and "number of rooms" sit next to each other in the same table.</p><p>If two columns carry almost the same information, unpenalised regression faces a dilemma: it cannot decide which one to give the weight to. Giving one 4 and the other 0 produces almost the same prediction as giving each 2. The model chooses <b>arbitrarily</b> between them.</p><p>The consequence: change the data a little and the coefficients swing completely. The model may be good at prediction but <b>its coefficients are meaningless</b>; its answer to "which variable matters how much" is unreliable.</p><p>And as the coefficients grow the model also becomes more sensitive to noise. The cure for both problems is the same, and that is what this lesson is about.</p>',
  'ridge:2': '<p>Ridge adds a single term to the objective of least squares:</p><p style="text-align:center"><b>RSS + λ · (the sum of the squared coefficients)</b></p><p>The model no longer says only "make the error small", it says "make the error small <b>but do not grow the coefficients either</b>". λ is the bargaining power between those two wishes.</p><p>To see what happens with correlated columns, the algebra is enough. Load the same total onto a single coefficient and its square is large; split it in two and the sum of the squares <b>halves</b>. Because the penalty sums squares, it <b>rewards sharing</b>.</p><p>The result: ridge splits the weight between correlated features and the coefficients become stable. It zeroes none of them and shrinks all of them.</p>',
  'ridge:3': '<p>This step shows one of the most counter-intuitive ideas in the course: <b>making a model deliberately worse in order to make it better.</b></p><p>As λ grows the model fits the training data worse; the training error rises. And yet the test error falls.</p><p>The reason is the bias-variance trade-off. The penalty adds a little <b>bias</b> to the model (it can no longer seek the best fit) but in return erases much more <b>variance</b> (the coefficients become less sensitive to changes in the data). The total error falls.</p><p>If λ grows too far it turns around: the penalty crushes the real signal too, the model pulls everything towards zero and the error rises again. So there is a <b>sweet spot</b>, and finding it is what this whole family is about.</p>',
  'ridge:4': '<p>How will you find the right λ? The answer in this step is short but requires discipline.</p><p>First eliminate the wrong routes. <b>You cannot look at the training error</b>, because it is always smallest at λ=0; that criterion always takes you to the unpenalised model and erases the whole benefit of ridge.</p><p><b>You cannot look at the test set either</b>; the moment you do, it stops being an honest measurement.</p><p>One legitimate route remains: <b>cross validation</b>. Split the training data into folds, measure the error on the unseen folds for every λ, and pick the best. So λ is not <i>learned</i> from the data, it is <i>searched for</i> with the data.</p><p>There is one more condition, and skipping it is a common mistake: because the penalty is applied at the same magnitude to all the coefficients, the features must be <b>scaled first</b>. A feature measured in metres and one measured in kilometres cannot take the same penalty.</p>',

  'lasso:1': '<p>Ridge shrank the coefficients but killed none of them. Lasso changes that with a single modification: it uses <b>the absolute value instead of the square</b> in the penalty.</p><p>That small looking difference changes the behaviour fundamentally, and the reason is hidden in the <b>derivative</b>.</p><p>The derivative of β² at zero is <b>zero</b>. So as a coefficient approaches zero the penalty\'s pushing force weakens; this is why ridge takes coefficients close to zero but never quite there.</p><p>The derivative of |β| at zero is <b>constant</b>. The penalty pushes with the same force right to the last moment and slams the coefficient to exactly zero.</p><p>The result: lasso produces <b>sparse</b> solutions. Some coefficients become exactly zero and those features leave the model.</p>',
  'lasso:2': '<p>Making a coefficient exactly zero is not just a mathematical detail; in practice it means <b>feature selection</b>.</p><p>A feature whose coefficient is zero contributes nothing to the model\'s output, that is it has been dropped. So lasso does two jobs at once: it regularises and it tells you which variables are needed.</p><p>The data in this step was generated deliberately, so <b>the right answer is known</b>: which features are genuine and which are noise is fixed in advance. That lets us compare lasso\'s selection against the truth rather than judging it by eye. You can only measure a method honestly in a setup where you know the right answer.</p><p>Its behaviour on correlated columns also differs from ridge: where ridge <b>shares</b> the weight between the two, lasso <b>picks</b> one and discards the other.</p>',
  'lasso:3': '<p>Now the real question: which is better?</p><p>The answer depends on the criterion, and that is one of the recurring themes of this course. If you want only <b>predictive power</b>, on correlated data ridge is usually ahead, because it shares the information rather than throwing it away.</p><p>But lasso gives you something else in return: <b>a short, defensible model.</b> A model that keeps twelve of two hundred features is far easier to defend in front of a regulator than a list of two hundred items.</p><p>So the choice is not technical but contextual: are you saying "give me the best prediction" or asking "which variable matters"?</p><p>There is also a method that combines the two: <b>elastic net</b> uses both penalties. It selects a correlated group together and discards the noise as well.</p>',

  'norm-l1l2:1': '<p>This lesson shows <b>why</b> the previous two behaved as they did. The answer is not in the algebra but in the <b>geometry</b>.</p><p>First the point of view has to change. Instead of "minimise RSS + λ·penalty" you can equally say: <b>minimise RSS, subject to the penalty not exceeding a certain budget.</b> Those two statements are two faces of the same problem; every value of λ has a corresponding budget.</p><p>Written as a budget, <b>a region</b> appears: the area the coefficients have to stay inside. And the shape of that region depends on the penalty. <b>The L2 budget is a circle</b> (a fixed sum of squares) and <b>the L1 budget is a diamond</b> (a fixed sum of absolute values).</p><p>The solution is here: the constant error curves (ellipses) widen around the unpenalised solution, and <b>the point where they first touch the budget region</b> is the solution you are after.</p>',
  'norm-l1l2:2': '<p>Now the whole matter comes down to one observation: <b>a diamond has corners and a circle does not.</b></p><p>The corners of the diamond sit on the axes. Being on an axis means <b>the other coefficient is exactly zero</b>.</p><p>As an ellipse shrinks towards the diamond it most probably hits a <b>corner</b> rather than an edge, because a corner is pointed and much easier for the ellipse to touch. That is precisely why lasso produces exact zeros.</p><p>A circle has no corner. Wherever the ellipse touches it, that point is almost never exactly on an axis. This is why ridge\'s coefficients shrink but never vanish.</p><p>And the difference grows with dimension: in high dimensions the number of corners of the L1 budget rises rapidly and every one of them is on an axis.</p>',
  'norm-l1l2:3': '<p>Now we can turn the choice into a rule.</p><p><b>L2 (ridge):</b> shrinks all the coefficients and discards none. Shares the weight across correlated features. It has a closed form, is fast, and works even when there are more features than examples.</p><p><b>L1 (lasso):</b> zeroes coefficients out and selects features. Picks one member of a correlated group and discards the rest. It has no closed form; it needs an algorithm such as coordinate descent.</p><p>The decision rule is one sentence: <b>L1 if you expect sparsity, L2 if you do not.</b></p><p>"Expecting sparsity" means this: there are many features but only a few of them really matter. Genetic data is the classic example: tens of thousands of genes, a few hundred patients, and a handful of genes thought to be related to the disease. If all the features contribute a little and your only concern is prediction, L2 suits better.</p>',

  'yanlilik:1': '<p>This lesson splits a model\'s error into two separate parts. The distinction looks abstract but in practice it directly answers the question "what should I do".</p><p>Until now you always had a single training set. But that set was a random sample; had different data arrived on another day you would have a different table.</p><p>In this step <b>many training sets</b> are drawn from the same process and a separate model is fitted to each. Two questions become askable:</p><p><b>Bias:</b> how far is the <b>average</b> of those models from the truth? That is, is the model family flexible enough to do this job?<br><b>Variance:</b> how different are the models <b>from each other</b>? That is, how much does the result depend on which data you happened to get?</p><p>They are different questions and their treatments differ too.</p>',
  'yanlilik:2': '<p>When you raise the model complexity the two numbers move in <b>opposite directions</b>, and that is not a preference but a mathematical necessity.</p><p>As flexibility grows the model family widens and becomes able to imitate the true relationship: <b>the bias falls</b>.</p><p>But a flexible model also fits the noise of the particular examples it got. Given another sample it would draw a completely different curve: <b>the variance rises</b>.</p><p>You cannot shrink both at once. All you can do is find the point where the <b>sum</b> is smallest. That is the mechanism underneath the U curve you saw on Track 0; there you saw the curve, here you see the two components that make it.</p>',
  'yanlilik:3': '<p>The expected test error consists of exactly three parts:</p><p style="text-align:center"><b>error = bias² + variance + noise</b></p><p>The first two are under your control. You can move both by changing the model family, adding a penalty or collecting data.</p><p>The third is not. <b>Noise</b> is the randomness that comes from the data itself and that no model can explain: measurement error, unrecorded variables, genuine chance. It forms a <b>floor</b>: whatever model you build, your error cannot go below it.</p><p>Its practical value is large. It is where the answer to "can I make my model better" comes from on a project: if your error is already near the noise floor, what you need is not a better model but <b>better measurement</b> or <b>more data</b>. Time spent on the model will be wasted.</p>',
  'yanlilik:4': '<p>We could do the decomposition above because we knew the true function and could draw as much data as we liked from the same process. <b>In real life you have neither.</b></p><p>But you can diagnose, because the two illnesses have different symptoms.</p><p><b>High bias:</b> the training error and the test error are both high and <b>close to each other</b>. The model cannot even learn the data. Treatment: a more flexible model, better features.</p><p><b>High variance:</b> the training error is low and the test error high. There is a <b>gap</b> between them. Treatment: more data, a penalty term, a simpler model.</p><p>So <b>the gap between the training and the test error is the measurable trace of variance.</b> The ridge and lasso lessons were exactly the cure for that second illness: they add a little bias and erase a lot of variance.</p>',
  'boyut-laneti:1': '<p>In the k-NN lesson we relied on an assumption: <b>points that are close together have the same label.</b> This lesson shows what happens to that assumption in high dimensions.</p><p>Our intuition developed in two and three dimensions. There the difference between "near" and "far" is clear. As the dimension grows that difference is <b>erased</b>.</p><p>The reason: every new dimension adds its own contribution to the distance between two points. As the number of dimensions grows those contributions accumulate and converge to an average; in the end all the points start to look <b>roughly equally</b> far apart.</p><p>For k-NN the implication is severe: if everybody is at the same distance, "the nearest k neighbours" means almost <b>k random points</b>. The notion of nearness loses its meaning and the model loses the assumption it was built on.</p>',
  'boyut-laneti:2': '<p>Let us see the same problem from a second angle: how "local" is a neighbourhood?</p><p>k-NN, kernel smoothing and the deep branches of a decision tree all rest on the same logic: <b>look at the nearby points.</b> So how wide does a region have to be to cover a small share of the data, say a tenth?</p><p>In one dimension the answer is intuitive: a tenth of the axis. But as the dimension grows the share the region must cover on every axis <b>grows fast</b>, because volume grows exponentially with dimension and a small box covers nothing in a huge space.</p><p>In high dimensions "10% of the data" means scanning almost the entire space. So <b>nothing called locality is left</b>. Local methods stop being local and their bias grows.</p>',
  'boyut-laneti:3': '<p>The third angle is geometry\'s most counter-intuitive result: <b>in high dimensions the volume escapes to the surface.</b></p><p>Go a little inwards from every edge of a cube and define an "inner core". In two dimensions the core holds most of the volume. As the dimension grows the core\'s share <b>erodes exponentially</b>, because it is multiplied by the same ratio in every dimension.</p><p>The result: in a high dimensional dataset almost every point is at an extreme value on at least one axis. So <b>in practice there is no such thing as "an average example".</b></p><p>The effect on the model is direct: every new example most probably falls outside the training data. The model constantly has to <b>extrapolate rather than interpolate</b>, and extrapolation is always riskier.</p>',
  'boyut-laneti:4': '<p>Everything you have read so far is true, and yet machine learning works with data of thousands of dimensions. How?</p><p>Because <b>real data is not spread uniformly through the space.</b> Images of handwritten digits with 784 pixels are not everywhere in a 784 dimensional space; almost none of the random pixel heaps look like a digit. Real images gather on a much lower dimensional surface. That is called the <b>manifold assumption</b>.</p><p>So the curse is <b>a curse of the true dimension, not the measured one.</b> In this experiment the points were spread uniformly on purpose, so you are seeing the curse in its heaviest form.</p><p>The practical job follows: find that low dimensional surface. PCA, autoencoders, embeddings, feature selection with lasso; they all do the same job.</p><p>And do not forget: <b>collecting more data is no remedy here.</b> The number of examples needed to keep the same density grows exponentially with the dimension; no budget can keep up.</p>',

  'hiper-arama:1': '<p>How do you choose hyperparameters? The first method that comes to mind is a <b>grid search</b>: pick a few values for each setting and try every combination.</p><p>It sounds systematic but there is a hidden waste in it, and to see it you need to know what real hyperparameter surfaces look like.</p><p>Bergstra and Bengio showed in 2012 that on these surfaces the settings are <b>not equally important</b>. Usually one or two settings determine the result and the effect of the rest is negligible. In neural networks the learning rate is like that.</p><p>The grid\'s problem is exactly here: on a k×k grid <b>you train k² models but only try k different values of the setting that matters.</b> The remaining trials are the same important value tried again and again with different values of an unimportant setting.</p>',
  'hiper-arama:2': '<p>A random search trains the same number of models but does not trap the points in a grid.</p><p>The consequence is direct: <b>make n trials and you try n different values of the setting that matters.</b> On a grid that number is only √n.</p><p>The core of the idea: <b>you do not know in advance which setting matters.</b> A grid has to allocate equal resolution to every setting despite not knowing. A random search never has to make that call; whichever setting turns out to matter, it has tried n different values of it.</p><p>This is why, on the same compute budget, a random search typically gives a better result. The conclusion runs against intuition but the reason is geometry rather than luck.</p>',
  'hiper-arama:3': '<p>The grid\'s second problem is more insidious: <b>raising the budget does not guarantee a better result.</b></p><p>The reason: the new grid may not contain the good points of the old one. A denser grid tries different values and none of them may land in the good region. So a grid\'s performance depends not on the budget but on <b>the luck of alignment</b>.</p><p>A random search does not depend on that kind of luck. Every new trial is an independent sample; so as the budget grows its curve rises smoothly and never goes backwards.</p><p>That is exactly Bergstra and Bengio\'s practical advice, and it still holds: <b>random instead of grid.</b></p>',
  'hiper-arama:4': '<p>A random search solves <i>where</i> to look but still has one waste: <b>it gives every trial equal resources.</b></p><p>Yet a bad configuration usually reveals itself as bad very early. Training it to the end is compute thrown away.</p><p><b>Successive halving</b> fixes that: train all the candidates on a small budget, keep the best half, train the rest for longer, eliminate again. The total budget stays the same while the resources shift to the good candidates. <b>Hyperband</b> generalises the idea by also searching for the answer to "how early should I eliminate".</p><p><b>Bayesian optimisation</b> is a third level: it builds a surface model from the points tried and puts the next trial where it is most informative. That is, it uses what it has learned.</p><p>All three share the same condition: the choice must be made by <b>cross validation</b>, and the test set looked at only at the very end.</p>',

  'softmax:1': '<p>The last layer of a classification network produces one number per class. They are called <b>logits</b> or raw scores and they are not probabilities: they can be negative, exceed 1, and sum to anything.</p><p>For probabilities we need two conditions: every value between 0 and 1 and a total of exactly 1. <b>Softmax</b> does that by exponentiating every score and dividing by their sum.</p><p>Exponentiating serves two purposes. It turns negative numbers positive, and it <b>magnifies the differences</b>: a difference of 1 unit between scores turns into a ratio of about 2.7 in probability.</p><p>One important property: softmax <b>does not disturb the ordering</b>. The largest score gets the largest probability. That is where the name comes from; it is a "soft maximum", picking the largest while leaving a share to the others.</p>',
  'softmax:2': '<p>The standard loss for classification is <b>cross entropy</b>, and it looks at something surprisingly narrow: <b>only the probability you gave the correct class.</b></p><p>How you split the probability among the other classes does not matter at all. The loss is computed from one number: −log(the probability of the correct class).</p><p>The behaviour of the logarithm is decisive here. If you gave the correct class a high probability the loss is near zero. As the probability shrinks the loss grows fast and <b>goes to infinity</b> as it approaches zero.</p><p>So cross entropy <b>punishes being confident and wrong mercilessly.</b> That is not a coincidence but comes from information theory: −log p measures "how surprised am I when this event happens". If a model gives something a very low probability and it happens, the model was very surprised and it pays for it.</p>',
  'softmax:3': '<p>Why not use squared error (MSE)? At first sight it looks reasonable: the square of the difference between prediction and truth.</p><p>The answer is hidden not in the <b>size</b> of the loss but in its <b>derivative</b>. What determines learning is not the loss itself but its gradient.</p><p>When softmax and cross entropy are combined the gradient comes out remarkably clean: <b>prediction minus truth</b>. The more wrong the model is the larger the gradient, so learning is strongest exactly where it is needed most.</p><p>When softmax and MSE are combined the gradient is multiplied by the derivative of softmax. That derivative <b>goes to zero</b> when the model is confident (when the probability approaches 0 or 1). The result is perverse: when the model is confident and wrong the gradient is nearly zero, so it <b>learns least at the moment it is most wrong.</b></p><p>This is why classification uses cross entropy. The right question is not "which loss is larger" but <b>"which loss teaches better"</b>.</p>',
  'softmax:4': '<p>One more setting can be added to softmax: dividing the scores by a <b>temperature</b> (T) first.</p><p>Its effect: as T shrinks the differences grow and the distribution piles onto the highest score; the model looks very confident. As T grows the differences erode and the distribution flattens; the model looks hesitant.</p><p>The critical point: <b>the ordering never changes.</b> Temperature changes not what the model says but <b>how confident it looks</b>.</p><p>That setting has two well known uses. In language models the temperature is used like a <b>creativity dial</b>: a low T almost always picks the same word, a high T gives irrelevant tokens a chance too. And in <b>knowledge distillation</b> T is raised to get the "soft" answers of the teacher model, because a soft distribution carries information about the similarity between classes.</p>',

  'dagilim-kaymasi:1': '<p>This lesson deals with the most common way models collapse in production, and the setup has to be read carefully.</p><p>The true rule is a <b>curve</b>. But the training data is gathered in only a narrow part of that curve. In that narrow region the curve looks almost straight, so a linear model does the job perfectly well.</p><p>The model is not wrong. It is just <b>right for the world it has seen.</b></p><p>The hidden assumption: the data arriving in production will come from the same place. That assumption has a name (the i.i.d. assumption) and it is machine learning\'s quietest and most frequently broken one. It is written nowhere, it gives no warning, and it sits under every model.</p>',
  'dagilim-kaymasi:2': '<p>Now the production data shifts: the incoming examples move outside the training region, towards where the curve bends.</p><p>The linear boundary cannot follow the truth there and the accuracy collapses. What to note: <b>not a single parameter of the model changed.</b> Same code, same server, same model file. The only thing that changed is who turned up.</p><p>So what broke is not the model but <b>the model\'s assumption about the world</b>.</p><p>This situation is called <b>covariate shift</b>: the distribution of the inputs changed but the true relationship between input and label stayed the same.</p><p>In real life its counterpart is very concrete: a campaign brings in a new customer base, a sensor ages, a competitor changes its prices, a pandemic changes habits. The model stays the same and the world moves.</p>',
  'dagilim-kaymasi:3': '<p>So how will you notice this in production? The answer is harder than you would think, because <b>you cannot measure accuracy in production.</b></p><p>Accuracy needs the true label. And the label either arrives far too late (you learn months later whether a loan was repaid), never arrives (you can never know why a user did not click), or is expensive (expert labelling).</p><p>But there is a signal that needs no labels: <b>the distribution of the incoming inputs.</b> Feature means, standard deviations, category distributions, missing value rates. All of these are computed instantly and for free.</p><p>And the critical point: input shift raises the alarm <b>before</b> the accuracy collapses. This is why serious monitoring systems watch the input first; accuracy is also computed retrospectively as labels arrive, but a system that relies on it alone is blind.</p>',
  'dagilim-kaymasi:4': '<p>Shift is not one thing; there are three kinds and <b>their treatments differ</b>. So the diagnosis comes first.</p><p><b>Covariate shift:</b> the distribution of the inputs P(x) changed but P(y|x) is the same. That is what you saw in this lesson. Treatment: collect data from the new region or reweight the examples.</p><p><b>Label shift:</b> the proportion of the classes P(y) changed; the fraud rate rose, say. Treatment: update the threshold and the class weights. Most of the time there is no need to retrain the model.</p><p><b>Concept shift:</b> the relationship itself P(y|x) changed. The same input now gives a different result. Treatment: retrain with new labelled data. There is no other remedy.</p><p>In adversarial environments (spam, fraud, security) concept shift is the <b>rule</b> rather than the exception, because on the other side there is somebody trying to beat the model. There retraining is not maintenance but part of the system.</p>',

  'ozellik-onemi:1': '<p>"Which variable is the model looking at?" is a question both customers and regulators ask. The answer is not as easy as it seems.</p><p>The first method that comes to mind is to look at the coefficients: a large coefficient means an important feature, right?</p><p>No. There are three separate traps. <b>One:</b> a coefficient changes with <b>scale</b>; change the same feature from metres to kilometres and the coefficient grows a thousandfold while the importance has not changed. <b>Two:</b> in non-linear models there is no such thing as a coefficient. <b>Three:</b> and the most insidious, a coefficient <b>depends on the model</b>.</p><p>You will see the third in this step: two models fitted to the same data contradict each other about which variable matters. Neither is lying. Because <b>a coefficient is a property of the model, not of the data.</b></p>',
  'ozellik-onemi:2': '<p>There is a model-independent measure that gets past the coefficient\'s problems: <b>permutation importance</b>.</p><p>The idea is simple and elegant: <b>shuffle</b> a feature\'s values across the rows, then look at how much the test error degrades. Shuffling destroys the information in that column without removing the column. If the error degrades a lot, the model was leaning on that feature.</p><p>It has two advantages over the coefficient: it is unaffected by scale and it works with every kind of model, in a tree as well as in a neural network.</p><p>But what you will see in this step: <b>permutation importance depends on the model too.</b> The same feature gets very different importance in different models.</p><p>We changed the measure and the problem did not go away, because what we are measuring is still <b>what the model leans on</b>. "Which variable really determines the data" is a completely different question, and permutation importance does not answer it.</p>',
  'ozellik-onemi:3': '<p>With correlated features permutation importance has a particular trap.</p><p>If two columns carry almost the same information and you shuffle one, the model turns to the other and takes the information from there. The error barely degrades. The same happens in the other direction. The result: <b>each hides behind the other</b> and measuring them one at a time makes both look less important than they are.</p><p>The way to see it is to shuffle them <b>together</b>. Shuffled together, the degradation comes out noticeably larger than the sum of the individual degradations; the difference is exactly the amount of "hiding".</p><p>The practical rule follows: <b>shuffle correlated features as a group.</b> The question "how important is the income group" is both more meaningful and more stable than "how important is monthly income".</p>',
  'ozellik-onemi:4': '<p>The most important sentence of this lesson is here, and it is the rule most often violated in practice.</p><p><b>Feature importance answers "what is the model leaning on". It does not answer "what would I have to change in reality for the outcome to change".</b></p><p>The second is a <b>causal</b> question, and causality cannot be extracted from data alone. A feature may be the cause of the outcome or merely a <b>symptom</b> of it. Both get high importance in a model, and the importance measure cannot tell them apart.</p><p>The classic example: a hospital model assigning high importance to "how many visits were received in the patient\'s room". That does not mean increasing visits will make the patient better; the number of visits is most probably a symptom of the severity of the illness.</p><p>One more technical warning: permutation produces data points that never occur in reality. With correlated columns that means questioning the model in a region it was never trained on, and its predictions there are unreliable.</p><p>Three practical rules: say which model you computed the measure with, measure correlated features as a group, and run a separate study if you are going to make a causal claim.</p>',
  'fisher-lda:1': '<p>Dimensionality reduction is essentially a problem of <b>choosing a direction</b>.</p><p>Reducing two dimensional data to one means picking a line and <b>projecting</b> every point onto it. Every point goes from two numbers to one: its position along that line.</p><p>But which line? There are infinitely many options and the choice changes the result completely. A good direction separates the two classes; a bad one stacks them on top of each other and the information is lost irrecoverably.</p><p>The question of this lesson: <b>on what basis do we choose the direction?</b> There are two different answers and both look reasonable. Understanding the difference means understanding the basic distinction between supervised and unsupervised methods.</p>',
  'fisher-lda:2': '<p>The first answer is PCA\'s, and it asks one thing: <b>in which direction does the data spread most?</b></p><p>That is a reasonable criterion, because the most spread direction preserves the most information. PCA is unsupervised; it <b>never looks</b> at the labels.</p><p>The problem is exactly there. A data cloud can be long for two reasons: because the classes are far apart, or because each class is very spread out <b>within itself</b>. PCA cannot tell those apart, because telling them apart would require looking at the label.</p><p>What you see in this step is the second case: the length of the cloud comes not from the difference between the classes but from the noise within them. PCA finds the most spread direction and in that direction the two classes overlap completely.</p><p>The lesson: <b>the direction of most spread is not the direction that separates best.</b></p>',
  'fisher-lda:3': '<p>Fisher asks a different question and brings the labels into play: <b>how far apart are the class means and how narrow is the spread within each class?</b></p><p>The measure is a ratio: <b>J = (the squared difference of the means) / (the sum of the within-class spreads)</b>.</p><p>What is in the numerator and denominator matters. We want the numerator large: the class centres far apart. We want the denominator small: each class a narrow cloud within itself. So the winning direction is the one where "the means are apart but the clouds are narrow".</p><p>That is fundamentally different from PCA\'s measure. PCA looks at the <b>total</b> spread; Fisher divides the <b>between-class</b> spread by the <b>within-class</b> spread. The result: Fisher can pick a direction very different from PCA\'s, even nearly perpendicular to it.</p><p>It also has a closed form: w ∝ S<sub>W</sub><sup>−1</sup>(m₁ − m₂), the inverse of the within-class scatter matrix times the difference of the means.</p>',
  'fisher-lda:4': '<p>The two are not rivals; they answer different questions.</p><p><b>PCA is unsupervised.</b> It needs no labels. It suits compressing data, reducing noise, visualisation, or situations where there are no labels at all.</p><p><b>LDA is supervised.</b> It wants labels and targets discriminability directly. It is usually better for dimensionality reduction before classification.</p><p>LDA has two practical limits. First: it can produce at most <b>the number of classes minus one</b> dimensions; with two classes you get only a single direction. Second: in very high dimensions the within-class scatter matrix becomes singular and cannot be inverted.</p><p>The standard fix for both is the same and widely used in practice: <b>go down to a reasonable dimension with PCA first, then apply LDA.</b></p>',

  'uretici-ayirici:1': '<p>There are two fundamentally different ways of looking at the same classification problem, and the difference is in <b>which question the model asks</b>.</p><p>The <b>discriminative approach</b> asks one thing: where is the boundary that separates the classes? It learns P(class | data) directly and takes no interest in where the data came from. Logistic regression is like that.</p><p>The <b>generative approach</b> asks a bigger question: <b>how does each class produce its data?</b> That is, P(data | class) and P(class) are learned and then inverted with Bayes\' rule to classify. Naive Bayes is like that.</p><p>At first sight the generative approach looks like unnecessary work: why learn the whole distribution when finding the boundary would do? This lesson\'s answer: with little data that "unnecessary work" turns into an <b>advantage</b>.</p>',
  'uretici-ayirici:2': '<p>As the amount of data grows the winner changes, and that is a general result rather than a coincidence.</p><p>With little data the <b>generative model leads</b>. The reason: it has strong assumptions, so it has fewer parameters to learn and draws conclusions quickly from few examples.</p><p>As data grows the <b>discriminative model overtakes</b>. Its assumptions are weaker; at the start that is a disadvantage, but as data becomes plentiful it can go lower because it is not limited by a wrong assumption.</p><p>Ng and Jordan showed this as a general result in 2001: <b>the generative model converges faster but converges to a higher error level; the discriminative model starts slowly but goes lower.</b></p><p>So the two curves cross. Where the crossing is depends on the problem, but that it exists is general.</p>',
  'uretici-ayirici:3': '<p>Naive Bayes plateauing past a point is a concrete example of the bias-variance lesson.</p><p>The "naive" in its name comes from one very strong assumption: <b>given a class, the features are independent of each other.</b></p><p>That assumption is almost never true in real data; columns are related. What happens when it is false? The model can never see its own error, because by construction it cannot represent that relationship.</p><p>So adding data does not fix the error; it only <b>teaches the wrong model more precisely</b>. The error sticks at a floor and stays there.</p><p>That is exactly <b>bias</b>: the error that comes from the model\'s own constraint and does not close with data. Naive Bayes is low variance and high bias; logistic regression is high variance and low bias.</p>',
  'uretici-ayirici:4': '<p>The choice is not made on accuracy alone, because a generative model gives you things beyond accuracy.</p><p><b>It can generate new data.</b> Because P(data | class) is modelled you can sample from that distribution. A discriminative model cannot; all it has is a boundary.</p><p><b>It can cope with missing values.</b> If a feature is absent a generative model marginalises it out and carries on.</p><p><b>It notices outliers.</b> If the probability of an incoming example is low it can say "this does not look like the world I have seen". That connects directly to the monitoring problem from the distribution shift lesson.</p><p><b>It can be trained per class.</b> When a new class is added you build only that class\'s model and leave the others alone. In a discriminative model the whole boundary has to be retrained.</p><p>The decision rule: generative if there is little data, a frequently changing class list, outlier detection or missing features; discriminative if there is plenty of data and the sole aim is the highest accuracy.</p>',

  'spline:1': '<p>The best known way of making a curve flexible is to raise the polynomial degree. But a polynomial has a property that turns into a flaw: <b>a polynomial is a global model.</b></p><p>Global means this: change one coefficient and the <b>whole</b> curve changes. A bend at the right end of the curve also affects the behaviour at the left end.</p><p>The practical consequence is annoying. You raise the degree to catch a sharp peak in the middle of the data; as the degree rises the curve starts making unwanted oscillations at the <b>edges</b>. So fitting one region breaks another.</p><p>This is a classic problem shown by Runge in 1901: with equally spaced points the oscillation at the edges grows as the degree rises. Look only at the average error and you will miss it; you have to look at the <b>worst deviation</b>.</p>',
  'spline:2': '<p>A spline does the same job with a completely different logic: <b>pieces instead of one curve.</b></p><p>The curve is cut at points called <b>knots</b> and a separate cubic polynomial is fitted to each piece. Where the pieces meet they are joined so that the curve and its derivatives stay continuous, producing one smooth curve.</p><p>The critical difference: <b>a spline is a local basis.</b> A knot\'s coefficient affects only its own region. Adding a knot in the middle does not break the edges.</p><p>So on the same parameter budget a spline can put the flexibility <b>where it is needed</b>, while a polynomial has to spread the budget everywhere at once.</p><p>The cubic degree is not a coincidence: it is the lowest degree with a continuous second derivative, that is the cheapest curve that looks smooth to the eye.</p>',
  'spline:3': '<p>Splines are not magic; they are subject to the same bias-variance trade-off.</p><p>Past a certain point adding knots stops paying off. The reason is familiar: the <b>noise floor</b>. The model starts chasing the noise in the data rather than the true curve.</p><p>So the number of knots is a <b>hyperparameter</b> and its right value is chosen by cross validation rather than by eye.</p><p>There is also an alternative approach: a <b>smoothing spline</b>. It puts a knot at every data point but applies a <b>penalty</b> to the curvature. That is, instead of limiting the number of knots it limits how much the curve is allowed to bend.</p><p>The penalty coefficient λ plays exactly the same role as the λ in the ridge lesson. The same idea in different clothes: making flexibility expensive instead of forbidding it.</p>',

  'ozellik-muh:1': '<p>This lesson shows how to change the result without changing the model at all: <b>by changing the columns you give it.</b></p><p>A linear model, as the name says, can only build <b>linear combinations</b> of the columns you give it: a·x₁ + b·x₂ + c. It can add and scale, and nothing else.</p><p>So <b>it cannot derive relationships like a product, a ratio or a square root by itself.</b> If the true rule is the product of two features and you did not supply that product as a column, the relationship is somewhere the model cannot reach. However much data it gets, it will not find it.</p><p>Adding that column, on the other hand, brings the problem within the model\'s reach at the cost of a single parameter. That is where the power of feature engineering comes from: <b>making the problem fit the model instead of making the model bigger.</b></p>',
  'ozellik-muh:2': '<p>Some numbers are not numbers. The hour, the month, the day of the week, an angle, a wind direction: all of these are <b>cyclic</b>.</p><p>Give the hour as an integer between 0 and 23 and you have told the model: "there is a distance of 23 units between 23 and 0." In reality there is one hour between them. The model cannot correct that false information, because that is the definition it was given.</p><p>Adding a polynomial term (hour + hour²) partly helps, because now a peak or a trough can be built. But the two ends of a parabola know nothing of each other; the break at midnight remains.</p><p>The right solution is to encode the hour on a <b>circle</b>: sin(2π·hour/24) and cos(2π·hour/24). Now 23 and 0 really are close and the cycle closes.</p><p>The general principle here: <b>how you encode a feature is a claim you make to the model about that feature.</b> A wrong encoding is a wrong claim.</p>',
  'ozellik-muh:3': '<p>Scaling is not a "tidying step"; for some models it is a <b>requirement</b>.</p><p>The reason: in a model that looks at distance, the contribution of two columns is proportional to the magnitude of their units. If "number of children" is between 0 and 4 and "income" is in the tens of thousands, the distance between two points is determined almost entirely by income. You give two columns and the model sees one.</p><p><b>Standardisation</b> fixes that: it shifts and divides every column so its mean is 0 and its standard deviation 1. Now every column contributes equally to the distance.</p><p>Do not see this as an "improvement". Before scaling, the model was <b>answering the wrong question correctly</b>: "who is the nearest neighbour in income?"</p><p>Which models are affected? Everyone that looks at a distance or a penalty term: kNN, k-means, SVM, PCA, ridge and lasso. Ridge\'s λ·Σw² term also distributes the penalty according to the units of the columns, so an unscaled ridge is meaningless.</p>',
  'ozellik-muh:4': '<p>There is a fair objection: trees and gradient boosting can discover interactions on their own. So why bother?</p><p>The answer is not "can it discover" but <b>"at what cost does it discover"</b>. Because tree splits are perpendicular to the axes, a tree can only approximate a product surface with rectangular steps. It spends data and depth on every step and still cannot catch the exact product.</p><p>So <b>a flexible model is no substitute for the right feature; it builds an expensive approximation of it.</b> Given the right feature, the model expresses it exactly with a handful of parameters.</p><p>In the other direction the tree wins: <b>scaling is completely unnecessary for a tree.</b> A split looks for a threshold, not a distance; scale a column by a factor of a thousand and the threshold scales too and the result does not change.</p><p>The rule that follows: <b>which preprocessing is needed depends on the model.</b> There is no universal checklist; scaling is mandatory for kNN and pointless for a tree.</p>',

  'gam:1': '<p>An additive model is an intermediate form between a linear model and a black box: <b>y = a₀ + f₁(x₁) + f₂(x₂) + …</b></p><p>Every feature has its own curve. More <b>flexible</b> than a linear model, because the curves do not have to be straight. More <b>constrained</b> than a black box, because the features cannot mix: the effect of x₁ is independent of x₂.</p><p>Solving those curves at once is hard. <b>Backfitting</b> does this trick: hold all the other curves fixed, subtract their contribution from y, and fit a single curve to what remains. Then move to the next. And repeat.</p><p>That way a multidimensional fitting problem is broken into <b>one dimensional fitting steps</b>. Because every round has to reduce the remaining error, the process settles into a decreasing sequence and converges quickly.</p>',
  'gam:2': '<p>The real gain of the additive structure is not in accuracy but in <b>readability</b>.</p><p>Because the effect of x₁ is independent of x₂, the question "what happens when x₁ rises" has <b>a single answer</b>, and that answer can be drawn as a curve. The model holds no hidden behaviour anywhere else.</p><p>So <b>these few plots are the entire model.</b> In a black box that is not possible: there a feature\'s effect varies with the values of the other features, so it cannot be told with a single curve.</p><p>And this is not the price of flexibility. Fit a linear model to the same data and you get a far worse result; so the gain comes from the <b>curves</b>, not from interaction. An additive model gives you the flexibility you need and withholds the flexibility you do not.</p><p>The parameter count is small too: two curves and an intercept. Every coefficient is part of a curve and the curve can be drawn directly.</p>',
  'gam:3': '<p>This step shows numerically a recurring idea of the course: <b>the right assumption is free accuracy.</b></p><p>If the data really was generated additively, the additive model <b>carries that information from the start</b>. So it can spend its data on building a smooth curve.</p><p>A tree tries to build the same smooth curve out of axis aligned steps and spends data on every step. Despite being more flexible it falls behind, because flexibility has a price: variance.</p><p>So <b>flexibility is not a virtue in itself.</b> If the assumption a model carries fits the data it brings a gain; if it does not it brings a loss. A correct constraint lowers variance for free, because the bias paid in return is zero.</p><p>In the next step we measure the reverse: what happens when the assumption breaks?</p>',
  'gam:4': '<p>Now the data changes: the output comes entirely from an <b>interaction</b>, with no additive part at all.</p><p>Here the additive model does not merely fall behind, it <b>drops below the mean</b>. The reason is instructive: look at x₁ alone and the average of the output is flat, and the same for x₂. That is, <b>there is nothing to learn in either marginal distribution.</b></p><p>The model still has to fit flexible curves and the only material it has is noise. The result is memorisation.</p><p>In the previous step the opposite happened. The same two models, the same order, the reverse result. The only thing that changed was <b>the structure of the data</b>. That is why "which model is better" is meaningless on its own.</p><p>The route used in practice is somewhere in between: adding <b>a few selected interaction terms</b> to the additive model by hand. That way readability is largely preserved while the known interactions are captured.</p>',
/*SON*/
};
